---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def from '../calculators/rebar';
import { rebarGrid, REBAR } from '../lib/framing';
import { int } from '../lib/format';

const slabs = [[10, 10], [12, 12], [16, 20], [20, 20], [24, 24], [30, 30]].map(([l, w]) => ({
  l,
  w,
  ...rebarGrid({ lengthFt: l, widthFt: w, spacingIn: 12, edgeIn: 3, size: '#4', stockFt: 20, lapDiameters: 40, wastePct: 0 }),
}));
---

<CalcPage
  slug="rebar-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Rebar Calculator: Bars, Linear Feet & Weight for a Slab"
  description="Free rebar calculator for concrete slabs. Enter slab size, grid spacing, and bar size to get the number of bars, linear feet, lap splices, and weight of #3, #4, #5, or #6 rebar."
  h1="Rebar Calculator"
  updated="2026-09-26"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Lay out a rebar grid for a concrete slab: bars in each direction, linear feet, lap splices, bars to buy, and total
    weight.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how-to">How to calculate rebar for a slab</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Subtract the edge distance from each side so bars stay inside the concrete.</li>
    <li>Bars running the length = (slab width − 2 × edge) ÷ spacing + 1. Do the same the other way.</li>
    <li>Each bar is the slab dimension minus 2 × edge. If that's longer than a stock bar, add lap splices.</li>
    <li>Multiply linear feet by the bar's weight per foot for total pounds.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="example">Example: a 20 × 20 ft slab, #4 bar at 12 in</h2>
  <p>
    With 3 in edge distance, each bar is 19 ft 6 in. (240 − 6) ÷ 12 + 1 = 20 bars each way, 40 bars in all, or 780 linear
    feet. Each bar comes from one 20 ft stick, so you buy 40 bars. At 0.668 lb per foot, the steel weighs 521 lb.
  </p>

  <h2 id="table">Rebar for common slab sizes</h2>
  <p>#4 bar, 12 in grid, 3 in edge distance, 20 ft bars, no waste.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Slab</th><th class="num">Bars (both ways)</th><th class="num">Linear ft</th><th class="num">20 ft bars to buy</th><th class="num">Weight</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{slabs.map((s) => <tr><td>{s.l} × {s.w} ft</td><td class="num">{s.alongLength.count + s.alongWidth.count}</td><td class="num">{int(s.totalFt)}</td><td class="num">{s.sticks}</td><td class="num">{int(s.weightLb)} lb</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="sizes">Rebar sizes and weights</h2>
  <p>The bar number is its diameter in eighths of an inch.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Bar</th><th class="num">Diameter</th><th class="num">Weight per foot</th><th class="num">20 ft bar</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{REBAR.map((b) => <tr><td>{b.size}</td><td class="num">{b.diaIn} in</td><td class="num">{b.lbPerFt} lb</td><td class="num">{(b.lbPerFt * 20).toFixed(1)} lb</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Ordering the base under the slab too? <a href="/gravel-calculator/">Estimate the gravel base</a> in tons, or
    <a href="/cubic-yard-calculator/">convert any fill to cubic yards</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>What spacing should rebar be in a slab?</h3>
  <p>
    Residential slabs and driveways commonly use #3 or #4 bar at 12 to 18 inches on center each way, but the right size
    and spacing depend on the load, soil, and slab thickness. Follow your plans or local code.
  </p>
  <h3>How much should rebar overlap?</h3>
  <p>
    A common rule of thumb is 40 bar diameters: 20 inches for #4 bar. Structural drawings may specify a different lap, and
    engineered values take priority.
  </p>
  <p>Size the pour itself with the <a href="/concrete-calculator/">concrete calculator</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Bars per direction = floor((dimension − 2 × edge) ÷ spacing) + 1.</li>
    <li>Short bars are cut several to a stick; long runs use n bars with (n − 1) lap splices.</li>
    <li>Weights use ASTM A615 unit weights. Chairs, ties, and dowels are not included.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
